capabilities for paranormal research. This is a factual/technical question, not really needing tools, but could benefit from web search for specs. I can answer from general knowledge about Galaxy S10 specs, but to be safe on specs I could do a quick search. The S10 specs: accelerometer, gyro, geomagnetic (magnetometer), proximity, barometer, hall sensor, RGB light sensor, HR sensor (heart rate), SpO2 sensor, fingerprint (ultrasonic). Capabilities: can do EVP-like recording, EMF via magnetometer, EVP apps, ghost box apps via radio? S10 has no FM radio? Actually S10 in US does have FM radio chip? The S10 (Snapdragon US) has FM radio enabled on some carriers. Uncertain.</div><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Given casual question, I can answer directly with some structure. Maybe a quick web search to confirm sensor list. Let's do one search for accuracy.</div></div></div></div></div> <div class="scroll-edge-fade scroll-edge-fade-bottom" aria-hidden="true"></div><div class="scroll-edge-fade-measure-sentinel" aria-hidden="true"></div></div></div></div><div data-v-4f3bad81="" class="toolcall-flow__item"><div data-v-e3c80efd=""><div data-v-2b4f6ec0="" data-v-e3c80efd="" class="toolcall-container default is-inline-detail toolcall-web_search"><div data-v-2b4f6ec0="" class="toolcall-trigger is-interactive" role="button" tabindex="0" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="toolcall-detail-4"><div data-v-064cc47f="" data-v-2b4f6ec0="" class="toolcall-title-container default is-expandable"><div data-v-064cc47f="" class="toolcall-title-name line"><svg data-v-064cc47f="" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" role="img" class="iconify toolcall-title-name-icon" width="1em" height="1em" viewBox="0 0 1024 1024" name="Search"><path d="M491.52 155.648a348.16 348.16 0 0 1 270.86848 566.8864l123.82208 123.86304a36.864 36.864 0 1 1-52.10112 52.10112l-123.86304-123.82208A348.16 348.16 0 1 1 491.52 155.648z m0 622.592a274.432 274.432 0 1 0 0-548.864 274.432 274.432 0 0 0 0 548.864z" fill="currentColor"></path></svg> <span data-v-064cc47f="" class="toolcall-title-name-text">Searchï¼ˆ2 resultsï¼‰</span></div> <!----> <div data-v-064cc47f="" class="toolcall-title-container-extra"> <svg data-v-064cc47f="" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" aria-hidden="true" role="img" class="iconify toolcall-title-container-extra-arrow" width="1em" height="1em" viewBox="0 0 1024 1024" name="Right_b"><path d="M681.00096 549.23264a36.864 36.864 0 0 0-0.08192-52.0192L395.22304 211.47648a36.864 36.864 0 1 0-52.14208 52.10112l259.6864 259.72736-259.76832 261.69344a36.864 36.864 0 0 0 52.30592 51.93728l285.696-287.744z" fill="currentColor"></path></svg></div></div></div> <div data-v-897ffb8e="" data-v-faa3924f="" data-v-94e2edb6="" data-v-2b4f6ec0="" class="inline-tool-detail-collapse" id="toolcall-detail-4"><div data-v-897ffb8e="" class="resize-container enable-grow-animation" style="height: 0px; opacity: 0;"><div data-v-897ffb8e="" class="slot-container"></div></div> <!----></div></div></div></div><div data-v-4f3bad81="" class="toolcall-flow__item"><div data-v-8f1d918e="" data-v-e3c80efd="" class="toolcall-container thinking-container block-container is-flat-think" share-mode="false"><div data-v-064cc47f="" data-v-8f1d918e="" class="toolcall-title-container default is-expandable"><div data-v-064cc47f="" class="toolcall-title-name line"><svg data-v-064cc47f="" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" role="img" class="iconify toolcall-title-name-icon" width="1em" height="1em" viewBox="0 0 1024 1024" name="Dot"><path d="M512 384a128 128 0 1 1 0 256 128 128 0 0 1 0-256z" fill="currentColor"></path></svg> <span data-v-064cc47f="" class="toolcall-title-name-text">Evaluating Smartphone Apps for Ghost Hunting: Limitations &amp; Recommendations</span></div> <!----> <div data-v-064cc47f="" class="toolcall-title-container-extra"> <svg data-v-064cc47f="" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" aria-hidden="true" role="img" class="iconify toolcall-title-container-extra-arrow" width="1em" height="1em" viewBox="0 0 1024 1024" name="Right_b"><path d="M681.00096 549.23264a36.864 36.864 0 0 0-0.08192-52.0192L395.22304 211.47648a36.864 36.864 0 1 0-52.14208 52.10112l259.6864 259.72736-259.76832 261.69344a36.864 36.864 0 0 0 52.30592 51.93728l285.696-287.744z" fill="currentColor"></path></svg></div></div> <div data-v-897ffb8e="" data-v-8f1d918e="" class="toolcall-content"><div class="scroll-edge-fade scroll-edge-fade-top" aria-hidden="true"></div><div data-v-897ffb8e="" class="resize-container" style="height: 0px; opacity: 0;"><div data-v-897ffb8e="" class="slot-container"><div data-v-8766e0de="" data-v-8f1d918e="" class="markdown-container toolcall-content-text"><div data-v-8766e0de="" class="markdown"><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">The search results were limited but I know the S10 specs well. I can answer comprehensively. Galaxy S10 sensors (per Samsung official spec): Accelerometer, Barometer, Ultrasonic Fingerprint Sensor, Gyro Sensor, Geomagnetic Sensor, Hall Sensor, Heart Rate Sensor, Proximity Sensor, RGB Light Sensor, SpO2 sensor. Cameras: triple rear (12MP wide, 12MP tele, 16MP ultrawide), front 10MP. Microphone: stereo mics. No IR blaster (S10 lacks IR blaster â€” last Samsung with IR was Note 4/Note Edge era; S6 had it. S10 does NOT have IR blaster). No thermal camera. FM radio: S10 supports FM radio on Snapdragon US models (enabled on some carriers).</div><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Paranormal applications:</div><ul start="1"><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">EMF detection: magnetometer (geomagnetic sensor) â€” apps like EMF Detector. Weak (only ~Â±490 Î¼T range, low sample rate ~50-100Hz, 16-bit?), picks up wiring, phones, speakers â€” great for debunking, not a K-II/Trifield replacement. Tri-axis so you can compute magnitude.</div></li><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">EVP: internal mics â€” usable but narrow frequency response ~20Hz-20kHz, decent SNR; use a better external USB mic or dedicated digital recorder (Zoom, Tascam DR-05X) with 96kHz sampling for ultrasonic EVP capture (which phone mics can't do â€” they filter above ~20kHz; some recorders can do 96kHz giving 48kHz upper range).</div></li><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">EVP apps: "spirit boxes" are just random audio banks â€” the phone generates noise, nothing real. Real ghost box uses AM/FM radio sweep; phone can't sweep radio since its radio chip isn't accessible (no scan output). S10's FM radio only works with headphones as antenna and can't be swept by apps... actually apps can't access it. So real SB-7-style ghost box not possible on phone.</div></li><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">SLS camera: Structured Light Sensor â€” that requires an IR dot projector + IR camera (like Xbox Kinect). S10 has no depth/IR camera on rear (S10 5G had ToF VGA depth camera actually! S10 5G includes a 3D Depth Camera (Time of Flight, VGA) used for Live Focus Video and Quick Measure). The regular S10/S10+ do not. ToF could theoretically do rough depth mapping â€” apps exist using front camera? No front ToF on S10 (S10 5G has front ToF too, actually S10 5G has two front cameras including ToF). But Kinect-style skeletal tracking apps on Android use ML on RGB camera â€” that's what "SLS camera" apps actually do: pose estimation stick figures overlaid on camera feed. So S10 can run such apps via RGB camera; that's what most phone "SLS" apps do anyway. Real Kinect v1/v2 still better.</div></li><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">REM-Pod: not possible â€” no E-field sensor.</div></li><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Thermal: no.</div></li><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Geophone/vibration: accelerometer â€” can log vibration but low sensitivity; can detect footsteps-ish vibrations if placed on surface. Gyro/accel can be a crude seismograph.</div></li><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Barometer: detects air pressure changes â€” some investigators watch for pressure drops; pressure changes too slow/small to be useful, but barometer is actually sensitive (Â±1 hPa, can detect altitude ~0.5m). Some use it, scientifically dubious.</div></li><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Compass: magnetometer again.</div></li><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Light sensor: detect illumination changes (RGB light sensor) â€” some apps use camera for that instead.</div></li><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">IR blaster: absent â€” can't learn/control IR devices, and no IR illumination for night vision. Night vision apps amplify camera sensitivity â€” S10 rear cameras lack large pixels; you can use external IR illuminator + some cameras see slight near-IR. Phone cameras have IR-cut filter but often see 850nm dimly, 940 barely. Sony A7S etc are better. Some investigators remove IR filter.</div></li><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Audio: ultrasonic â€” mics don't capture above 20kHz typically (48kHz sampling). Bats/infrasound (infrasound mic + recording possible down to 20Hz; infrasound &lt;20Hz not captured). Dedicated infrasound recorders needed.</div></li><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Compass apps, "ghost radar" apps â€” entertainment random number generators.</div></li><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Communication/UX side: logging, notes, timestamped photos/video, full-spectrum video? No. Video recording with night mode, external IR light sources.</div></li><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Ovilus-like: apps use word banks/random word generation â€” not measuring anything.</div></li></ul><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Also note: phone itself generates EMF (cell radio, Wi-Fi, Bluetooth) â€” should be in airplane mode during sweeps.</div><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">I should be honest but not condescending â€” user is into paranormal research; I can present neutrally: what it can/can't do, plus debunking value.</div><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Format: use headings/bullets, moderately detailed. Cite the search results where used (ultrasonic fingerprint, heart rate sensor). Maybe 2 citations. Keep length reasonable but thorough given the question asks broadly.</div></div></div></div></div> <div class="scroll-edge-fade scroll-edge-fade-bottom" aria-hidden="true"></div><div class="scroll-edge-fade-measure-sentinel" aria-hidden="true"></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div> <!----> <!----></div> <div data-v-b2cba09c="" class="toolcall-rollup__tail is-open"><div data-v-b2cba09c="" class="toolcall-rollup__tail-in"><div data-v-b2cba09c="" class="toolcall-rollup__tail-divider"></div> <!----> <div data-v-8766e0de="" data-v-e3c80efd="" class="markdown-container"><div data-v-8766e0de="" class="markdown"><h1>Galaxy S10 for Paranormal Research: What It Can and Can't Do</h1><h2>Sensor Inventory (what you're actually working with)</h2><div class="paragraph" dir="auto"><span>The S10 packs a surprisingly good sensor suite for a 2019 phone: accelerometer, gyroscope, geomagnetic (magnetometer) sensor, barometer, proximity sensor, RGB light sensor, hall sensor, heart rate + SpO2 sensors, and an ultrasonic in-screen fingerprint reader that maps your print in 3D using sound waves.</span><div data-v-f6319aea="" data-v-e3c80efd="" class="pua-ref-renderer pua-ref-renderer--2"><a data-v-c45f46df="" href="https://www.cnet.com/tech/mobile/galaxy-s10-has-ultrasonic-fingerprint-scanner-heres-why-you-should-care-explainer/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer noopener nofollow" class="pua-ref-cite-tag pua-ref-cite-tag--text" data-site-name="CNET"><!----></a></div><span> The S10 was also the last Galaxy S to include the dedicated heart rate sensor on the back.</span><div data-v-f6319aea="" data-v-e3c80efd="" class="pua-ref-renderer pua-ref-renderer--2"><a data-v-c45f46df="" href="https://www.reddit.com/r/samsunggalaxy/comments/1ltpfsi/remember_when_phones_used_to_have_this/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer noopener nofollow" class="pua-ref-cite-tag pua-ref-cite-tag--text" data-site-name="reddit.com"><!----></a></div><span> Plus triple rear cameras (wide/tele/ultrawide), stereo mics, and a decent speaker.</span></div><h2>What It CAN Be Used For</h2><div class="paragraph" dir="auto"><strong>EMF detection (sort of).</strong> The magnetometer works with free apps (e.g., "EMF Detector," "Ultimate EMF") to read the local magnetic field in microteslas, tri-axis, and log it. This is genuinely useful â€” mostly for <em>debunking</em>. It will spike near wiring, appliances, speakers, and the phone's own radios (use airplane mode). It's nowhere near a K-II, Trifield, or Mel meter in sensitivity, sample rate, or range, but for finding wiring-in-walls explanations, it works.</div><div class="paragraph" dir="auto"><strong>Audio/EVP recording.</strong> The stereo mics record clean 48kHz audio â€” fine for standard EVP sessions. Record, timestamp, and review later; long battery life and storage help. But it's a mid-tier recorder at best (see limitations below).</div><div class="paragraph" dir="auto"><strong>Camera documentation.</strong> Photos/video with timestamps, night mode for low-light stills, and the ultrawide lens is handy in tight locations. Great for baseline documentation and "did something move?" comparisons.</div><div class="paragraph" dir="auto"><strong>"SLS" stick-figure apps.</strong> Real SLS (Structured Light Sensor) rigs use an IR dot projector + IR camera, like a Kinect. Phone "SLS" apps actually do skeletal pose-estimation on the regular RGB camera feed using AI. The S10 runs these fine â€” but understand it's the same software trick on any phone, not a true depth sensor. (If you have the S10 <strong>5G</strong>, its rear ToF depth camera adds real depth data, but still not Kinect-grade.)</div><div class="paragraph" dir="auto"><strong>Vibration/geophone-ish logging.</strong> The accelerometer can act as a crude seismograph to log knocks, footsteps, or furniture movement if the phone sits on a surface. Low sensitivity, but real data.</div><div class="paragraph" dir="auto"><strong>Barometer watching.</strong> Some investigators log air pressure. The sensor is accurate, though pressure changes from "entities" are, let's say, scientifically unproven â€” treat it as logging, not evidence.</div><div class="paragraph" dir="auto"><strong>Field logistics.</strong> GPS-tagged notes, voice memos, app-based session logs, red-light screen filters to preserve night vision, and a flashlight. Honestly this is where a phone shines most on an investigation.</div><h2>What It CANNOT Do (the honest list)</h2><ul start="1"><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto"><strong>True EMF measurement.</strong> The magnetometer only senses the <em>magnetic</em> field component. It can't measure electric fields, RF, or AC gauss the way dedicated meters do. Phones also emit their own EMF â€” always airplane mode.</div></li><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto"><strong>Real spirit box sweeps.</strong> An SB-7/ghost box works by rapidly sweeping live AM/FM radio. The S10's FM tuner isn't accessible to apps for sweeping (and needs wired headphones as an antenna anyway). Any app that plays random audio snippets is just a random-word/noise generator â€” it's producing the "answers" itself.</div></li><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto"><strong>High-sample-rate EVP.</strong> Phone mics top out around 20kHz and use lossy processing. For ultrasonic EVP work (recording at 96kHz to capture up to 48kHz), you need a dedicated recorder like a Tascam DR-05X or Zoom. Same for infrasound below 20Hz â€” phones can't capture it at all.</div></li><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto"><strong>Night vision / IR illumination.</strong> No IR blaster, no thermal camera. The rear cameras have an IR-cut filter, so they see near-IR only faintly. Pairing the phone with an external 850nm IR illuminator gets you dim monochrome-ish video, but a real IR camcorder or a used Kinect blows it away.</div></li><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto"><strong>REM-Pod-style E-field detection.</strong> No such sensor exists in the phone.</div></li><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto"><strong>Ovilus-style "real" word output.</strong> Apps that speak words are RNG-driven word banks. They're entertainment unless a device genuinely samples EMF/env data â€” and phone sensors aren't wired into those apps in any meaningful way.</div></li></ul><h2>Bottom Line</h2><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">The S10 is a solid <strong>secondary/documentation tool</strong>: magnetometer for debunking, recorder for standard EVP, camera for evidence logging, and accelerometer for vibration logging. It is <strong>not</strong> a replacement for dedicated gear â€” a K-II/Trifield for EMF, a 96kHz recorder for EVP, a Kinect for real SLS, and an IR camcorder for night vision. If you tell me which specific tools you're trying to substitute for (or what apps you're considering), I can give more targeted advice.</div></div></div></div></div></div> <!----> <!----> <!----></div>  <!----> <!----> <!----> <!----> <!---->   <!----> <div data-v-0754fef1="" class="okc-cards-container"><!----> <!----> <!----> <!----> <!----></div> <!----> <!----> <!----> <!----> <div data-v-ae31b4b4="" data-v-aaf8281b="" class="segment-assistant-actions"><div data-v-ae31b4b4="" class="segment-assistant-actions-content"> <!----> <!----> <!----><div data-v-ab0641ea="" data-v-ae31b4b4="" class="icon-button" style="width: 28px; height: 28px;"><svg data-v-ab0641ea="" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" role="img" class="iconify" width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 1024 1024" name="Copy"><path d="M725.333333 302.933333a166.4 166.4 0 0 1 166.4 166.4v256a166.4 166.4 0 0 1-166.4 166.4h-256A166.4 166.4 0 0 1 302.933333 725.333333v-256A166.4 166.4 0 0 1 469.333333 302.933333h256z m-256 76.8A89.6 89.6 0 0 0 379.733333 469.333333v256c0 49.493333 40.106667 89.6 89.6 89.6h256