a106.666667 106.666667 0 0 0 71.125333-100.096 35.584 35.584 0 1 0-71.125333 0 35.584 35.584 0 0 1-71.082667 0 35.584 35.584 0 1 0-71.125333 0 106.666667 106.666667 0 0 0 71.125333 100.096V725.333333h-47.018667a307.029333 307.029333 0 0 0-87.04-137.984 248.874667 248.874667 0 0 1 169.941334-430.933333 246.101333 246.101333 0 0 1 165.376 63.232 248.661333 248.661333 0 0 1-1.706667 372.693333h-0.085333z" fill="currentColor"></path></svg> <span data-v-064cc47f="" class="toolcall-title-name-text">Run LLMs on S10 via DeX and Cloud Agents</span></div> <!----> <div data-v-064cc47f="" class="toolcall-title-container-extra"> <svg data-v-064cc47f="" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" aria-hidden="true" role="img" class="iconify toolcall-title-container-extra-arrow" width="1em" height="1em" viewBox="0 0 1024 1024" name="Right_b"><path d="M681.00096 549.23264a36.864 36.864 0 0 0-0.08192-52.0192L395.22304 211.47648a36.864 36.864 0 1 0-52.14208 52.10112l259.6864 259.72736-259.76832 261.69344a36.864 36.864 0 0 0 52.30592 51.93728l285.696-287.744z" fill="currentColor"></path></svg></div></div> <div data-v-897ffb8e="" data-v-8f1d918e="" class="toolcall-content"><div class="scroll-edge-fade scroll-edge-fade-top" aria-hidden="true"></div><div data-v-897ffb8e="" class="resize-container" style="height: 0px; opacity: 0;"><div data-v-897ffb8e="" class="slot-container"><div data-v-8766e0de="" data-v-8f1d918e="" class="markdown-container toolcall-content-text"><div data-v-8766e0de="" class="markdown"><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Merlin is responding to my warning about Android killing Termux. He says Termux stays alive as long as he stays on the Termux screen (foreground app), and he uses cloud models (FreeBuff, OpenRouter) not local models since the phone can't run local models well.</div><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Short reply: acknowledge â€” foreground = no killing, that's correct; screen-on requirement is the cost (battery, screen burn-in, can't use the phone for other things). Suggest keeping it foreground/docked, maybe use an old phone/dedicated device, or Samsung DeX to free the phone. Also note that local models on S10 â€” 8GB, llama.cpp could run 1-2B quant or 3B Q4 slowly, but his point stands; cloud agents are the way.</div><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Keep it brief and conversational, maybe a couple of practical tips: wakelock still useful for doze when screen off briefly, keep plugged in (S10 supports 15W, fine), DeX mode lets you use a monitor/keyboard and Termux stays foreground â€” actually DeX is a great suggestion for running coding agents on the S10. Also screen burn-in on AMOLED â€” dark theme, lower brightness.</div></div></div></div></div> <div class="scroll-edge-fade scroll-edge-fade-bottom" aria-hidden="true"></div><div class="scroll-edge-fade-measure-sentinel" aria-hidden="true"></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div> <!----> <!----></div><div data-v-b2cba09c="" class="toolcall-rollup__part toolcall-rollup__flat"><!----> <div data-v-8766e0de="" data-v-e3c80efd="" class="markdown-container"><div data-v-8766e0de="" class="markdown"><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Right â€” foreground changes everything. If Termux is the app on screen, Android treats it as the active process and the OOM killer leaves it alone. My warning applies to background/screen-off operation; your setup sidesteps it entirely by keeping the agents in front. That tracks with what actually happens on Samsung devices.</div><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">The tradeoffs to plan around, then:</div><div class="paragraph" dir="auto"><strong>Screen-on is the tax.</strong> AMOLED burn-in is real if Termux sits static for hours every day â€” dark theme, low brightness, and maybe a screensaver rotation if you walk away. Battery too: agents running hours on end want the phone plugged in, and the S10's 15W charging will roughly hold steady against a Termux-plus-wifi load. It'll run warm, not hot.</div><div class="paragraph" dir="auto"><strong>DeX is worth a look.</strong> If the S10 ever sits at a desk, DeX gives you the monitor/keyboard experience and Termux runs properly in a window â€” agents stay foreground, and you get real copy-paste and a readable screen for reviewing diffs. Honestly the most comfortable way to babysit a swarm. It also means the phone can stay parked and charging while you drive it like a tiny workstation.</div><div class="paragraph" dir="auto"><strong>And agreed on local models.</strong> The S10's 8GB could technically limp a 1â€“2B quantized model at a few tokens a second, but that's a toy, not a coding agent. Cloud-side (FreeBuff/OpenRouter) is the correct call â€” the phone is a terminal and orchestrator, the compute lives elsewhere. That's also why your architecture holds up: even the cheapest part of the chain is doing no heavy lifting.</div><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">So the working setup: S10 on the Termux screen running Hermes against cloud models, GitHub as the state buffer, native sensor service alongside (it'll survive fine since Termux holds focus and the sensor app runs as a foreground service), computer pulling from both. The one gap in that picture is the sensor service â€” that's still the native Kotlin piece that needs to exist. Want to sketch that WebSocket protocol next, or get the repo skeleton down first?</div></div></div></div> <div data-v-b2cba09c="" class="toolcall-rollup__tail"><div data-v-b2cba09c="" class="toolcall-rollup__tail-in"><!----></div></div></div> <!----> <!----> <!----></div>  <!----> <!----> <!----> <!----> <!---->   <!----> <div data-v-0754fef1="" class="okc-cards-container"><!----> <!----> <!----> <!----> <!----></div> <!----> <!----> <!----> <!----> <div data-v-ae31b4b4="" data-v-aaf8281b="" class="segment-assistant-actions"><div data-v-ae31b4b4="" class="segment-assistant-actions-content"> <!----> <!----> <!----><div data-v-ab0641ea="" data-v-ae31b4b4="" class="icon-button" style="width: 2