ingest, verify manifest</span></div> <!----> <div data-v-064cc47f="" class="toolcall-title-container-extra"> <svg data-v-064cc47f="" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" aria-hidden="true" role="img" class="iconify toolcall-title-container-extra-arrow" width="1em" height="1em" viewBox="0 0 1024 1024" name="Right_b"><path d="M681.00096 549.23264a36.864 36.864 0 0 0-0.08192-52.0192L395.22304 211.47648a36.864 36.864 0 1 0-52.14208 52.10112l259.6864 259.72736-259.76832 261.69344a36.864 36.864 0 0 0 52.30592 51.93728l285.696-287.744z" fill="currentColor"></path></svg></div></div> <div data-v-897ffb8e="" data-v-8f1d918e="" class="toolcall-content"><div class="scroll-edge-fade scroll-edge-fade-top" aria-hidden="true"></div><div data-v-897ffb8e="" class="resize-container" style="height: 0px; opacity: 0;"><div data-v-897ffb8e="" class="slot-container"><div data-v-8766e0de="" data-v-8f1d918e="" class="markdown-container toolcall-content-text"><div data-v-8766e0de="" class="markdown"><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">The user pasted the Notebook's search results â€” a nicely formatted report of MCP server candidates, plus a recommendation of hybrid ADB + WebSocket. The message ends with a question apparently from Notebook: "Would you like me to draft an initial manifest.json schema or write an ingest script in Python to test the SHA-256 hashing and directory auto-sorting?"</div><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">I should respond to this. The user pasted it presumably for my reaction/review. I should evaluate the results, agree/disagree, and answer the pending question â€” probably both: draft the manifest.json schema AND the ingest script, or do one now. I can actually create the deliverables using ipython: write manifest.json schema and a Python ingest script, test it, and save to /mnt/agents/output/ as deliverables with file REF tags.</div><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Good plan:</div><ol start="1"><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Brief review of Notebook's results (endorse hybrid recommendation).</div></li><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Produce: manifest.json schema (JSON Schema file), ingest.py that auto-sorts incoming files into case dirs with SHA-256 + manifest update, plus a quick test run proving it works.</div></li><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Save as deliverables in /mnt/agents/output/ with REF tags.</div></li></ol><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Let me write the code. Keep it reasonably compact but real.</div><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Design:</div><ul start="1"><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">cases/&lt;case-uuid&gt;</div></li><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">ingest.py:</div><ul start="1"><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">new_case(name, base_dir) â†’ creates structure, manifest.json with schema_version, case_id, name, created, status: open, artifacts: []</div></li><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">ingest(case_id, src_path, kind, source) â†’ classify kind if not given by extension; compute sha256; rename; move; append manifest entry {id, type, original_name, stored_as, sha256, ingested_at, source}; atomic manifest write (tmp + replace); optionally git add/commit if in repo.</div></li><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">close_case(case_id) â†’ status closed, write manifest final, zip archive.</div></li></ul></li><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">manifest.json schema file (JSON Schema draft 2020-12).</div></li></ul><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Test: create temp case, ingest a fake wav, a jpg, a csv, verify manifest entries and hashes, close case. Then copy final files to /mnt/agents/output/.</div><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Deliverables: ingest.py, manifest.schema.json. Maybe also a sample manifest generated. Keep response text concise, then REF tags at end.</div><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Let me write it.</div></div></div></div></div> <div class="scroll-edge-fade scroll-edge-fade-bottom" aria-hidden="true"></div><div class="scroll-edge-fade-measure-sentinel" aria-hidden="true"></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div> <!----> <!----><!----> <div data-v-8766e0de="" data-v-e3c80efd="" class="markdown-container"><div data-v-8766e0de="" class="markdown"><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Notebook did well â€” the hybrid call is the right one. ADB MCPs handle the lifecycle stuff they were actually built for (screenshots, pulling clips, launching apps), and they don't pretend to do high-rate sensor streaming, which is exactly where they'd fall apart. I'd adopt that split as-is.</div><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">On its closing question: both. The schema and the ingest script are the same ~100 lines of logic anyway, and testing them against each other is the fastest way to find the cracks. Let me build and run it.</div></div></div><div data-v-4f3bad81="" data-v-f179e12b="" class="toolcall-flow is-collapsible"><div data-v-4f3bad81="" class="toolcall-flow__summary-clp is-open"><div data-v-4f3bad81="" class="toolcall-flow__summary-in"><div data-v-4f3bad81="" class="toolcall-flow__summary-wrap"><button data-v-4f3bad81="" type="button" class="toolcall-flow__summary" aria-expanded="false" aria-label="Expand tool calls"><svg data-v-4f3bad81="" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" aria-hidden="true" role="img" class="iconify toolcall-flow__summary-icon" width="1em" height="1em" viewBox="0 0 1024 1024" name="Document"><path d="M660.48 661.205333a38.4 38.4 0 0 1 0 76.8H363.52a38.4 38.4 0 0 1 0-76.8h296.96zM526.336 473.6a38.4 38.4 0 0 1 0 76.8H363.52a38.4 38.4 0 0 1 0-76.8h162.773333z" fill="currentColor"></path><path d="M562.005333 89.6c32.810667 0 64.298667 13.056 87.466667 36.266667l163.370667 163.285333c23.210667 23.210667 36.266667 54.698667 36.266666 87.509333V768A166.4 166.4 0 0 1 682.666667 934.4H341.333333A166.442667 166.442667 0 0 1 174.933333 768V256c0-91.904 74.496-166.4 166.4-166.4h220.672zM341.333333 166.4A89.6 89.6 0 0 0 251.73